// python-rt.metajs -- PYTHON'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the NINTH and last language after the Lua
// pilot, PHP, Swift, Java, C#, Dart, Go and Ruby.
//
// languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 86 js_* externs over
// the full-syntax ratchet. 32 of them are generic primitives languages/lib/runtime.c
// already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 54 are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/python-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-python-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/python-rt.ll that python-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol, so
// nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are deliberately
// named py*, pyA*..pyF*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrt.go (Python has NO dedicated Go twin:
// its semantics live in the shared file, in the js_py* extern table and the py*
// helper functions - pyString / pyRepr / pyTypeName / pyEqual / pyMRO / pyLookup /
// pyGetAttr / pySetAttr / pyMethodCall / pyBindCall / pyArith / pyFormat / memberCall)
// plus abnf/jsrtregexpy.go for the `re` module. This file matches them function for
// function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in full.
//
// ----- the value model -------------------------------------------------------
//
//     null / undefined            ==  None
//     true / false                ==  True / False
//     a PLAIN NUMBER              ==  int when it is integral and finite, else float.
//                                     There is no box: pyTypeName decides by
//                                     Math.trunc, exactly like abnf/jsrt.go:9332.
//     {__pybig, neg, limbs}       ==  an arbitrary precision int (js_bigint). Go uses
//                                     math/big; the base-10000 limbs are chunk A's.
//     a string                    ==  str
//     an array                    ==  list AND tuple (Python's tuple has no separate
//                                     representation in either half)
//     {__dict, keys, vals}        ==  dict, INSERTION ordered, parallel arrays
//     {__set, elems}              ==  set, duplicate free by pyEqual
//     {__cplx, real, imag}        ==  complex
//     {__mro, __name, __super}    ==  a class object; __mro is the C3 linearization
//     {__class: <class>}          ==  an instance
//     a function                  ==  a function (typeof "function"; tag 16, a
//                                     generator FUNCTION, answers that too since the
//                                     floor's type_of learned t == 16)
//     a tag-15 cell               ==  a generator. There is NO tag test in layer 2,
//                                     so pyIsGen below is STRUCTURAL - the floor
//                                     answers exactly one member on a generator,
//                                     "next" (runtime.c:2868), and a cursor cannot be
//                                     stored on one, which is php's finding.
//
// ----- what Python needed from the floor, and what it did NOT ----------------
//
//  1. NO sized-integer tag and NO boxed double. Python's int and float are the same
//     plain double, discriminated by Math.trunc, so neither tag 13 nor tag 14 is
//     reached from this file. The si_norm question ("is the language's unboxed
//     representation the one si_norm produces") does not arise. Python's ARBITRARY
//     PRECISION int does, and the answer is a base-10000 bignum in MetaJS rather
//     than a floor tag: Go reaches math/big through *jsBigInt and the floor has no
//     equivalent, so chunk A's pyABig* is the whole of it.
//  2. `__raw` IS needed, and Python is only the third language that needs it (lua
//     and php are the others). `grep -n truthyExt languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf`
//     answers `core.truthyExt = "js_pytruthy"`, so compile-core's truthy() emits
//     `icmp ne <call>, 0` on THIS FILE's function and consumes the result as a RAW
//     INTEGER. js_pytruthy is therefore written as js_pytruthy__raw.
//  3. A coroutine primitive IS used: `grep -c 'js_genfn\|js_yield'
//     languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf` is 6. Python is the first language on the
//     floor's generators that needed layer-2 lines for them - the emitter drives
//     js_genfn/js_yield itself (csharp's zero-line finding), but the INSPECTION
//     surface (next / send / close / drain / len) is Python's own and is here.
//  4. NO jsChar. Python has no character type and the emitter emits js_char /
//     js_char_code / js_kindex zero times.
//
// ----- the scope chain: lowered in the EMITTER, not here ---------------------
//
// SIX externs took a scope handle - js_pyfnscope, js_pyglobal, js_pynonlocal,
// js_pyset_var, js_pydel_var and js_pyannot - plus js_pyclass_fill, which enumerated
// one. A scope is the C floor's PRIVATE storage, so none of them could be written
// here, and runtime.c says so itself at js_pyset_var ("A Python layer 2 will have to
// add them"). They are lowered in python-to-llvm-ir.abnf instead, over
// js_scope_new / js_scope_decl / js_scope_get / js_scope_set / js_scope_typeof,
// which BOTH engines have. That is the seventh confirmation of the
// private-representation rule and by far the largest: six externs left the module,
// where php lowered 2, swift 2, java 1, csharp 0, dart 2, go 1 and ruby 1.
//
// ----- the regular-expression engine is IMPORTED, not copied ------------------
//
// languages/lib/regex.js is the shared backtracking engine that every INTERPRETER
// grammar includes into its tag script, and abnf/jsrtregex.go is its line-by-line Go
// port. It is written in the same MetaJS this file is, so layer 2 needs no third
// copy - ruby found this and python is the language it was found FOR.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the TYPE
// CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one branch and an
// object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI, so every
// `return` carries its value on the same line and a value-less early return is
// BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its
// from-index, array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.
// There is no hasOwn in a -rt-lib module, so Go's `v, ok := o.props[n]` is spelled
// `o[n] !== undefined` throughout; at RUNTIME an object is a floor cell with no
// prototype, so the Object.prototype hazard of the compiler's own goja objects does
// not arise here.

import "./regex.js"

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). It supplies
// the externs every language implements identically, parameterised by the rtk*
// hooks below. Same mechanism ruby uses for `import "./regex.js"`.
import "./runtime.metajs"

// ----- the rtk* hooks the shared runtime calls -------------------------------
function rtkStr(v) { return pyAJavaStr(v) }
function rtkNum(v) { return pyNum(v) }
function rtkIsChar(v) { return false }
function rtkIsArr(v) { return pyIsArr(v) }

// ===================================================== the value model ======

function pyIsObj(v) {
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    return !Array.isArray(v)
}

function pyIsArr(v) { return Array.isArray(v) }
function pyIsStr(v) { return typeof v == "string" }
function pyIsNum(v) { return typeof v == "number" }
function pyIsBool(v) { return typeof v == "boolean" }
function pyIsFn(v) { return typeof v == "function" }
function pyIsCallable(v) { return typeof v == "function" }

function pyIsNone(v) {
    if (v === null) { return true }
    return v === undefined
}

function pyTag(v, name) {
    if (!pyIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return v[name] === true
}

function pyIsDict(v) { return pyTag(v, "__dict") }
function pyIsSet(v) { return pyTag(v, "__set") }
function pyIsCplx(v) { return pyTag(v, "__cplx") }
function pyIsBig(v) { return pyTag(v, "__pybig") }

// A class object carries its C3 linearization; an instance carries its class.
function pyIsClass(v) {
    if (!pyIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return v["__mro"] !== undefined
}

function pyIsInst(v) {
    if (!pyIsObj(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__mro"] !== undefined) { return false }
    return pyIsObj(v["__class"])
}

// A GENERATOR is a floor cell (tag 15). Layer 2 has no tag test, so the question is
// answered structurally: the floor answers exactly ONE member on a generator, "next"
// (runtime.c:2868), and none of the objects this value model builds carries a
// callable "next" of its own.
function pyIsGen(v) {
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__dict"] === true) { return false }
    if (v["__set"] === true) { return false }
    if (v["__cplx"] === true) { return false }
    if (v["__pybig"] === true) { return false }
    if (v["__mro"] !== undefined) { return false }
    if (v["__class"] !== undefined) { return false }
    return typeof v["next"] == "function"
}

function pyIsIntVal(v) {
    if (pyIsBig(v)) { return true }
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    if (v !== v) { return false }
    if (v - v !== 0) { return false }
    return v == Math.trunc(v)
}

function pyFail(msg) { fail(msg) }

// ===================================================== dict =================
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's dictParts / dictFind / dictAppend. A dict KEY is compared with
// STRICT equality (Go's strictEq through dictFind), not with Python ==; that is the
// Go twin's choice and it is reproduced rather than repaired.

function pyNewDict() { return js_dict_new() }

function pyDictFind(keys, k) {
    for (var i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
        if (keys[i] === k) { return i }
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

function pyDictHas(d, k) {
    if (!pyIsDict(d)) { return false }
    return pyDictFind(d["keys"], k) >= 0
}

function pyDictGet(d, k) {
    if (!pyIsDict(d)) { return undefined }
    var i = pyDictFind(d["keys"], k)
    if (i < 0) { return undefined }
    return d["vals"][i]
}

function pyDictSet(d, k, v) {
    var keys = d["keys"]
    var i = pyDictFind(keys, k)
    if (i >= 0) {
        d["vals"][i] = v
        return d
    }
    keys.push(k)
    d["vals"].push(v)
    return d
}

// ===================================================== set ==================
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's newPySet / pySetElems / pySetHas / pySetAdd. MEMBERSHIP is Python
// ==, so 1 and 1.0 are the same member - which is not the strict equality the dict
// keys above use, and that asymmetry is the Go twin's.

function pyNewSet(elems) { return {__set: true, elems: elems} }

function pySetElemsOf(v) {
    if (!pyTag(v, "__set")) { return null }
    var e = v["elems"]
    if (!Array.isArray(e)) { return null }
    return e
}

function pySetHas(els, v) {
    for (var i = 0; i < els.length; i++) {
        if (pyEqual(els[i], v)) { return true }
    }
    return false
}

function pySetAdd(els, v) {
    if (!pySetHas(els, v)) { els.push(v) }
    return els
}

// ===================================================== complex ==============
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's newPyComplex / pyComplexParts: a plain number reads as (n, 0), so
// mixed arithmetic needs no second code path.

function pyNewCplx(re, im) { return {__cplx: true, real: re, imag: im} }

function pyCplxRe(v) {
    if (pyIsCplx(v)) { return pyNum(v["real"]) }
    return pyNum(v)
}

function pyCplxIm(v) {
    if (pyIsCplx(v)) { return pyNum(v["imag"]) }
    return 0
}

// ===================================================== conversions ==========

// rt.toNumber. `v * 1` IS ToNumber: the floor's js_mul coerces both operands the
// same way abnf/jsrt.go's toNumber does, so undefined answers NaN, null 0, a bool
// 1/0, a string its parse and EVERY object (a __pybig and a __cplx included) NaN -
// which is what Go's `default:` arm gives, and what `**`, `//` and `%` on a big or a
// complex depend on.
function pyNum(v) { return v * 1 }

// rt.toString. The one arm `"" + v` does not give is the bignum, whose Go twin
// answers big.Int.String(); pyABigStr (chunk A) is that, and top-level function
// declarations hoist, so it is reachable from here.
function pyToStr(v) {
    if (pyIsBig(v)) { return pyABigStr(v) }
    return "" + v
}

// pyStrLen counts CODE POINTS (abnf/jsrt.go:9773), where the string indexing of
// js_pyget counts UTF-16 code units. That inconsistency is the Go twin's and is
// reproduced rather than repaired.
function pyStrLen(s) {
    var n = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charCodeAt(i)
        if (c >= 55296 && c <= 56319 && i + 1 < s.length) { i = i + 1 }
        i = i + 1
        n = n + 1
    }
    return n
}

function pyStrAt(s, i) {
    var n = 0
    var k = 0
    while (k < s.length) {
        var c = s.charCodeAt(k)
        var w = 1
        if (c >= 55296 && c <= 56319 && k + 1 < s.length) { w = 2 }
        if (n == i) { return s.substring(k, k + w) }
        k = k + w
        n = n + 1
    }
    return ""
}

// ===================================================== rendering ============
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's pyString (2467) and pyRepr (2458), one for one.

function pyStr(v) {
    if (v === null) { return "None" }
    if (v === undefined) { return "None" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") {
        if (v) { return "True" }
        return "False"
    }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) {
        var out = "["
        for (var i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0) { out = out + ", " }
            out = out + pyRepr(v[i])
        }
        return out + "]"
    }
    if (pyIsObj(v)) {
        var els = pySetElemsOf(v)
        if (els != null) {
            var so = "{"
            for (var j = 0; j < els.length; j++) {
                if (j > 0) { so = so + ", " }
                so = so + pyRepr(els[j])
            }
            return so + "}"
        }
        if (pyIsDict(v)) {
            var ks = v["keys"]
            var vs = v["vals"]
            var d = "{"
            for (var k = 0; k < ks.length; k++) {
                if (k > 0) { d = d + ", " }
                d = d + pyRepr(ks[k]) + ": " + pyRepr(vs[k])
            }
            return d + "}"
        }
    }
    return pyToStr(v)
}

// pyString is the Go NAME of the function above; some ported blocks call it that.
function pyString(v) { return pyStr(v) }

function pyRepr(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return "'" + v + "'" }
    return pyStr(v)
}

// abnf/jsrt.go's pyTypeName (9324). A __pybig is an int, which is why the arm is
// ahead of the object arms - Go reaches it earlier because *jsBigInt is not a
// *jsObject and here it IS one.
function pyTypeName(v) {
    if (v === null) { return "NoneType" }
    if (v === undefined) { return "NoneType" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return "bool" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return "str" }
    if (pyIsBig(v)) { return "int" }
    if (typeof v == "number") {
        if (v !== v) { return "float" }
        if (v - v !== 0) { return "float" }
        if (v == Math.trunc(v)) { return "int" }
        return "float"
    }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return "list" }
    if (typeof v == "function") { return "function" }
    if (pyIsObj(v)) {
        if (pySetElemsOf(v) != null) { return "set" }
        if (pyIsDict(v)) { return "dict" }
        return "object"
    }
    return "object"
}

// ===================================================== equality and order ===
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's pyEqual (8580). The bigint arms are chunk A's pyABigCmp /
// pyABigEqFloat; they are called from here so every caller of pyEqual gets them.

function pyEqual(x, y) {
    var xs = pySetElemsOf(x)
    if (xs != null) {
        var ys = pySetElemsOf(y)
        if (ys == null) { return false }
        if (xs.length != ys.length) { return false }
        for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) {
            if (!pySetHas(ys, xs[i])) { return false }
        }
        return true
    }
    if (pySetElemsOf(y) != null) { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(x)) {
        if (!Array.isArray(y)) { return false }
        if (x.length != y.length) { return false }
        for (var j = 0; j < x.length; j++) {
            if (!pyEqual(x[j], y[j])) { return false }
        }
        return true
    }
    if (pyIsDict(x)) {
        if (!pyIsDict(y)) { return false }
        var xk = x["keys"]
        var yk = y["keys"]
        if (xk.length != yk.length) { return false }
        for (var k = 0; k < xk.length; k++) {
            var p = pyDictFind(yk, xk[k])
            if (p < 0) { return false }
            if (!pyEqual(x["vals"][k], y["vals"][p])) { return false }
        }
        return true
    }
    if (pyIsBig(x)) {
        if (pyIsBig(y)) { return pyABigCmp(x, y) == 0 }
        if (typeof y == "number") { return pyABigEqFloat(x, y) }
    }
    if (pyIsBig(y)) {
        if (typeof x == "number") { return pyABigEqFloat(y, x) }
    }
    if (pyIsCplx(x)) {
        if (pyIsCplx(y)) {
            if (pyCplxRe(x) != pyCplxRe(y)) { return false }
            return pyCplxIm(x) == pyCplxIm(y)
        }
        if (pyCplxIm(x) != 0) { return false }
        return pyCplxRe(x) == pyNum(y)
    }
    if (pyIsCplx(y)) {
        if (pyCplxIm(y) != 0) { return false }
        return pyCplxRe(y) == pyNum(x)
    }
    return x === y
}

// abnf/jsrt.go's jsCompare: -1 / 0 / 1, and 2 for a NaN operand, which is what makes
// <, >, <= and >= all false at once. The bigint pair arm lives in chunk A's
// pyACompare, which wraps this one.
function pyCompare(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string") {
        if (typeof r == "string") {
            if (l < r) { return 0 - 1 }
            if (l > r) { return 1 }
            return 0
        }
    }
    var a = pyNum(l)
    var b = pyNum(r)
    if (a !== a) { return 2 }
    if (b !== b) { return 2 }
    if (a < b) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (a > b) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// ===================================================== classes =============
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's pyClassObj / pyInstance / pyMRO / pyLookup / pyIsDescriptor /
// pyGetAttr / pyInstanceDict / pySetAttr / pySlotAllows / pyAttrError.

function pyClassOf(v) {
    if (pyIsClass(v)) { return v }
    return null
}

function pyInstClassOf(v) {
    if (!pyIsObj(v)) { return null }
    if (v["__mro"] !== undefined) { return null }
    var c = v["__class"]
    if (!pyIsObj(c)) { return null }
    return c
}

// The resolution order of a class, itself first. A class object that predates the
// MRO (an older {__name, __super} exception descriptor) answers with its __super
// chain, so the two shapes stay interchangeable.
function pyMRO(c) {
    var out = []
    var m = c["__mro"]
    if (Array.isArray(m)) {
        for (var i = 0; i < m.length; i++) {
            if (pyIsObj(m[i])) { out.push(m[i]) }
        }
        return out
    }
    var k = c
    while (pyIsObj(k)) {
        out.push(k)
        k = k["__super"]
    }
    return out
}

function pyLookupHas(c, name) {
    if (name == "") { return false }
    var mro = pyMRO(c)
    for (var i = 0; i < mro.length; i++) {
        if (mro[i][name] !== undefined) { return true }
    }
    return false
}

function pyLookupVal(c, name) {
    if (name == "") { return undefined }
    var mro = pyMRO(c)
    for (var i = 0; i < mro.length; i++) {
        if (mro[i][name] !== undefined) { return mro[i][name] }
    }
    return undefined
}

// pyIsDescriptor: an instance whose class defines the given hook.
function pyDescriptor(v, hook) {
    var cls = pyInstClassOf(v)
    if (cls == null) { return undefined }
    var m = pyLookupVal(cls, hook)
    if (typeof m != "function") { return undefined }
    return m
}

// An instance's own attributes as a Python dict (pyInstanceDict). The __-prefixed
// slots are internal and are skipped, which is what Go does with inst.keys.
function pyInstanceDict(inst) {
    var d = pyNewDict()
    var ks = keysOf(inst)
    for (var i = 0; i < ks.length; i++) {
        var k = ks[i]
        if (k.length >= 2 && k.charAt(0) == "_" && k.charAt(1) == "_") { continue }
        d["keys"].push(k)
        d["vals"].push(inst[k])
    }
    return d
}

function pyGetAttr(obj, name) {
    if (pyIsClass(obj)) {
        if (name == "__name__") { return obj["__name"] }
        if (name == "__mro__") { return obj["__mro"] }
        if (pyLookupHas(obj, name)) { return pyLookupVal(obj, name) }
        fail("type object '" + pyToStr(obj["__name"]) + "' has no attribute '" + name + "'")
    }
    var cls = pyInstClassOf(obj)
    if (cls != null) {
        if (name == "__class__") { return cls }
        if (name == "__dict__") { return pyInstanceDict(obj) }
        if (obj[name] !== undefined) { return obj[name] }
        if (pyLookupHas(cls, name)) {
            var v = pyLookupVal(cls, name)
            var get = pyDescriptor(v, "__get__")
            if (get !== undefined) { return get.apply(undefined, [v, obj, cls]) }
            return v
        }
        fail("'" + pyToStr(cls["__name"]) + "' object has no attribute '" + name + "'")
    }
    if (name == "__name__") {
        // A synthetic class object for a BUILTIN type (what type(3) hands out)
        // carries only its name, and a function carries none of its own - the name
        // its def bound it under was recorded by js_pyfnname (chunk B).
        if (pyIsObj(obj)) {
            if (obj["__name"] !== undefined) { return obj["__name"] }
        }
        var fn = pyBFuncName(obj)
        if (fn !== undefined) { return fn }
    }
    return pyGetMember(obj, name)
}

// rt.getMember for the shapes this value model builds. A dict answers its own
// members, an array its length, a string its length, and anything else the plain
// property.
function pyGetMember(obj, name) {
    if (typeof obj == "string") {
        if (name == "length") { return obj.length }
        return undefined
    }
    if (Array.isArray(obj)) {
        if (name == "length") { return obj.length }
        return undefined
    }
    if (obj === null) { fail("cannot read '" + name + "' of None") }
    if (obj === undefined) { fail("cannot read '" + name + "' of None") }
    return obj[name]
}

function pySlotAllows(slots, name) {
    if (Array.isArray(slots)) {
        for (var i = 0; i < slots.length; i++) {
            if (pyToStr(slots[i]) == name) { return true }
        }
        return false
    }
    if (typeof slots == "string") { return slots == name }
    return true
}

function pySetAttr(obj, name, v) {
    var cls = pyInstClassOf(obj)
    if (cls != null) {
        if (pyLookupHas(cls, name)) {
            var cur = pyLookupVal(cls, name)
            var set = pyDescriptor(cur, "__set__")
            if (set !== undefined) {
                set.apply(undefined, [cur, obj, v])
                return v
            }
        }
        if (pyLookupHas(cls, "__slots__")) {
            if (!pySlotAllows(pyLookupVal(cls, "__slots__"), name)) {
                throw pyExcInstance("AttributeError",
                                    "'" + pyToStr(cls["__name"]) + "' object has no attribute '" + name + "'")
            }
        }
        obj[name] = v
        return v
    }
    if (pyIsClass(obj)) {
        obj[name] = v
        return v
    }
    if (pyIsObj(obj)) {
        obj[name] = v
        return v
    }
    fail("cannot set '" + name + "' on a " + pyTypeName(obj))
    return v
}

// ===================================================== builtin classes ======
//
// pyBuiltinClass hands out ONE stable class object per builtin type name, so
// type(x) is type(y) holds for two values of the same builtin type. The table key is
// "$"-prefixed, which is the intern-key rule of docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md.

var pyTypeTable = {}

function pyBuiltinClass(name) {
    var k = "$" + name
    var got = pyTypeTable[k]
    if (got !== undefined) { return got }
    var c = {__name: name}
    pyTypeTable[k] = c
    return c
}

// abnf/jsrt.go's pyExcInstance: the class is a stable per-name object deriving from
// one called Exception, so the name walk of pyIsInstance answers `except Exception`
// (and `except <ThatName>`) for a StopIteration / UnboundLocalError / AttributeError
// the RUNTIME raised, exactly like for one the program raised.
function pyExcInstance(name, msg) {
    var cls = pyBuiltinClass(name)
    if (name != "Exception") {
        if (cls["__super"] === undefined) { cls["__super"] = pyBuiltinClass("Exception") }
    }
    return {__class: cls, args: [msg]}
}

function pyRaiseExc(name, msg) { throw pyExcInstance(name, msg) }

// ===================================================== calling ==============

function pyCall(f, args) { return f.apply(undefined, args) }

function pyCallSelf(f, self, args) {
    var a = [self]
    for (var i = 0; i < args.length; i++) { a.push(args[i]) }
    return f.apply(undefined, a)
}

// ===================================================== sequences ============
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's pySeqElems (8759): the element list of anything a slice assignment
// may take on its right hand side. A GENERATOR is DRAINED, which is the one arm that
// has to be written structurally here (see pyIsGen).

function pyDrainGen(g) {
    var out = []
    var nx = g["next"]
    while (true) {
        var st = nx.apply(g, [])
        if (st === undefined) { return out }
        if (st["done"] === true) { return out }
        out.push(st["value"])
    }
    return out
}

function pySeqElems(v) {
    var els = pySetElemsOf(v)
    if (els != null) {
        var c = []
        for (var i = 0; i < els.length; i++) { c.push(els[i]) }
        return c
    }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) {
        var a = []
        for (var j = 0; j < v.length; j++) { a.push(v[j]) }
        return a
    }
    if (pyIsGen(v)) { return pyDrainGen(v) }
    if (typeof v == "string") {
        var s = []
        var n = pyStrLen(v)
        for (var k = 0; k < n; k++) { s.push(pyStrAt(v, k)) }
        return s
    }
    fail("cannot assign a " + pyTypeName(v) + " to a slice")
    return []
}

// ===================================================== slices ===============
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's pySliceRange (2432), pyClampIndex (8729) and pySliceIndices (8699).

function pySliceClamp(v, dflt, n) {
    if (v === undefined) { return dflt }
    if (v === null) { return dflt }
    var i = Math.trunc(pyNum(v))
    if (i < 0) { i = i + n }
    if (i < 0) { i = 0 }
    if (i > n) { i = n }
    return i
}

function pySliceRange(lo, hi, n) {
    var from = pySliceClamp(lo, 0, n)
    var to = pySliceClamp(hi, n, n)
    if (to < from) { to = from }
    return [from, to]
}

function pyClampIndex(i, n, step) {
    var k = i
    if (k < 0) {
        k = k + n
        if (k < 0) {
            if (step < 0) { return 0 - 1 }
            return 0
        }
        return k
    }
    if (k >= n) {
        if (step < 0) { return n - 1 }
        return n
    }
    return k
}

function pySliceIndices(n, loV, hiV, stepV) {
    var step = 1
    if (!pyIsNone(stepV)) {
        step = Math.trunc(pyNum(stepV))
        if (step == 0) { fail("slice step cannot be zero") }
    }
    var lo = 0
    var hi = n
    if (step < 0) {
        lo = n - 1
        hi = (0 - n) - 1
    }
    if (!pyIsNone(loV)) { lo = pyClampIndex(Math.trunc(pyNum(loV)), n, step) }
    if (!pyIsNone(hiV)) { hi = pyClampIndex(Math.trunc(pyNum(hiV)), n, step) }
    var out = []
    var i = lo
    while (true) {
        if (step > 0) {
            if (!(i < hi)) { return out }
        } else {
            if (!(i > hi)) { return out }
        }
        if (i >= 0 && i < n) { out.push(i) }
        i = i + step
    }
    return out
}
// chunkA.metajs -- CHUNK A of languages/lib/python-rt.metajs: NUMBERS,
// OPERATORS and CONVERSIONS, plus the arbitrary precision integer.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrt.go, not its comments. Every block
// below names the Go function it ports. Where the port CANNOT be exact the site
// says so in full.
//
// Ported here:
//     js_pybin      abnf/jsrt.go:7540   (reaching pySetBin, pyComplexBin,
//                                        pyDunderBin, pyArith, pyFloorMod,
//                                        pyContains, pySeqRepeat, jsAdd,
//                                        jsCompare, pyEqual, toInt32)
//     js_pyunary    abnf/jsrt.go:7757
//     js_pyint      abnf/jsrt.go:7741
//     js_pyfloat    abnf/jsrt.go:7994
//     js_pybool     abnf/jsrt.go:7981
//     js_pycomplex  abnf/jsrt.go:7359
//     js_pysum      abnf/jsrt.go:8000
//     js_pymax / js_pymin   abnf/jsrt.go:7651 -> pyMinMax (jsrt.go:9557)
//     js_pyrange    abnf/jsrt.go:7142
//     js_pyeq       abnf/jsrt.go:7179
//     js_jadd       abnf/jsrt.go:5151
//     js_bigint     abnf/jsrt.go:5366
//
// js_lt is a FLOOR extern and is deliberately absent.

// ============================================================================
// The arbitrary precision integer   (Go: *jsBigInt over math/big, jsrt.go:2561,
// bigPair jsrt.go:2710, bigArith jsrt.go:2724, bigEqFloat jsrt.go:8650)
// ============================================================================
//
// The representation is {__pybig: true, neg: <bool>, limbs: [...]}: the
// MAGNITUDE as a LITTLE-ENDIAN array of base-10000 limbs and a separate sign, so
// every intermediate of the schoolbook algorithms below stays under 10^8 and a
// double holds it EXACTLY. The shape of the limb arithmetic is lifted from
// languages/lib/ruby-rt.metajs (rbBigFromNum / rbBigMulSmall / rbBigStr).
//
// Zero is canonical: limbs == [0] and neg == false, so pyABigCmp needs no
// negative-zero case.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED, and why:
//
//   * A bignum SHIFT and a bignum BITWISE and/or/xor. Go never has one either:
//     js_pybin's "|" "&" "^" "<<" ">>" arms run rt.toInt32, and toInt32 goes
//     through rt.toNumber, whose *jsBigInt arm does not exist - it falls into
//     the default and answers NaN, hence 0. A big meeting a bit operator is 0 in
//     Go and is 0 here.
//
//   * A bignum <-> double CONVERSION. Same reason: Go's rt.toNumber has NO
//     *jsBigInt case, so `bigval // 2`, `bigval % 2`, `bigval ** 2`, float(big)
//     and int(float) of a big all see NaN in Go. Reproducing that only needs the
//     PRELUDE's pyNum to answer NaN for a plain object, which it does; nothing
//     is needed here. pyABigFromNum below is the one direction that IS needed
//     (bigEqFloat), and it is exact.
//
//   * Base-0 literal parsing. Go's js_bigint calls big.Int.SetString(s, 0),
//     which also accepts 0x / 0o / 0b prefixes and a leading-zero OCTAL. The one
//     caller, makeIntLit in languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf:1216, reaches
//     js_bigint ONLY on the radix-10 branch with the '_' separators already
//     stripped, so pyABigFromStr accepts a sign and decimal digits only and
//     fails loudly on anything else - exactly where Go's `!ok` fails.
//
//   * Karatsuba / Knuth-D. The multiplication is O(n*m) schoolbook and the
//     division is a per-limb BINARY SEARCH for the quotient digit (14 compares a
//     limb). The reachable operands are Python integer literals, so this is
//     small and exact rather than fast.

function pyABigNorm(limbs) {
    var out = limbs
    while (out.length > 1 && out[out.length - 1] == 0) { out.pop() }
    if (out.length == 0) { out.push(0) }
    return out
}

function pyABigNew(neg, limbs) {
    var ls = pyABigNorm(limbs)
    var n = neg
    if (ls.length == 1 && ls[0] == 0) { n = false }
    return {__pybig: true, neg: n, limbs: ls}
}

function pyABigIsZero(v) {
    var ls = v["limbs"]
    return ls.length == 1 && ls[0] == 0
}

// |a| <=> |b| over two NORMALIZED limb arrays.
function pyABigCmpMag(a, b) {
    if (a.length != b.length) { return a.length < b.length ? -1 : 1 }
    var i = a.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) { return a[i] < b[i] ? -1 : 1 }
        i = i - 1
    }
    return 0
}

// The signed comparison - Go's big.Int.Cmp, which jsCompare and pyEqual use.
function pyABigCmp(x, y) {
    if (x["neg"] != y["neg"]) { return x["neg"] ? -1 : 1 }
    var c = pyABigCmpMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])
    if (x["neg"]) { return -c }
    return c
}

function pyABigAddMag(a, b) {
    var out = []
    var carry = 0
    var n = a.length
    if (b.length > n) { n = b.length }
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        var av = i < a.length ? a[i] : 0
        var bv = i < b.length ? b[i] : 0
        var s = av + bv + carry
        if (s >= 10000) {
            carry = 1
            s = s - 10000
        } else {
            carry = 0
        }
        out.push(s)
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (carry > 0) { out.push(carry) }
    return out
}

// |a| - |b|, and the caller guarantees |a| >= |b|.
function pyABigSubMag(a, b) {
    var out = []
    var borrow = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var bv = i < b.length ? b[i] : 0
        var s = a[i] - bv - borrow
        if (s < 0) {
            s = s + 10000
            borrow = 1
        } else {
            borrow = 0
        }
        out.push(s)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return pyABigNorm(out)
}

// A magnitude times a single limb (0..9999), as a FRESH array: the division
// below needs the multiplicand left alone, unlike ruby-rt's in-place rbBigMulSmall.
function pyABigMulSmall(limbs, k) {
    var out = []
    var carry = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < limbs.length) {
        var v = limbs[i] * k + carry
        carry = Math.floor(v / 10000)
        out.push(v - carry * 10000)
        i = i + 1
    }
    while (carry > 0) {
        var c2 = Math.floor(carry / 10000)
        out.push(carry - c2 * 10000)
        carry = c2
    }
    return pyABigNorm(out)
}

function pyABigNeg(x) {
    var ls = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < x["limbs"].length) {
        ls.push(x["limbs"][i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return pyABigNew(!x["neg"], ls)
}

function pyABigAdd(x, y) {
    if (x["neg"] == y["neg"]) { return pyABigNew(x["neg"], pyABigAddMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])) }
    var c = pyABigCmpMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])
    if (c == 0) { return pyABigNew(false, [0]) }
    if (c > 0) { return pyABigNew(x["neg"], pyABigSubMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])) }
    return pyABigNew(y["neg"], pyABigSubMag(y["limbs"], x["limbs"]))
}

function pyABigSub(x, y) { return pyABigAdd(x, pyABigNeg(y)) }

function pyABigMul(x, y) {
    var a = x["limbs"]
    var b = y["limbs"]
    var out = []
    var z = 0
    while (z < a.length + b.length) {
        out.push(0)
        z = z + 1
    }
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var carry = 0
        var j = 0
        while (j < b.length) {
            var cur = out[i + j] + a[i] * b[j] + carry
            carry = Math.floor(cur / 10000)
            out[i + j] = cur - carry * 10000
            j = j + 1
        }
        var k = i + b.length
        while (carry > 0) {
            var cur2 = out[k] + carry
            carry = Math.floor(cur2 / 10000)
            out[k] = cur2 - carry * 10000
            k = k + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return pyABigNew(x["neg"] != y["neg"], pyABigNorm(out))
}

// |a| divided by |b| -> {q, r}, both NORMALIZED limb arrays. Schoolbook long
// division a limb at a time; the quotient limb is found by binary search over
// 0..9999 because there is no wide-multiply primitive to estimate it with.
// The caller has already rejected a zero divisor.
function pyABigDivMag(a, b) {
    var qms = []
    var rem = [0]
    var i = a.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        // rem = rem * 10000 + a[i], which in little-endian is a PREPENDED limb.
        var shifted = [a[i]]
        if (!(rem.length == 1 && rem[0] == 0)) {
            var t = 0
            while (t < rem.length) {
                shifted.push(rem[t])
                t = t + 1
            }
        }
        rem = pyABigNorm(shifted)
        var lo = 0
        var hi = 9999
        var d = 0
        while (lo <= hi) {
            var mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2)
            if (pyABigCmpMag(pyABigMulSmall(b, mid), rem) <= 0) {
                d = mid
                lo = mid + 1
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1
            }
        }
        qms.push(d)
        rem = pyABigSubMag(rem, pyABigMulSmall(b, d))
        i = i - 1
    }
    // qms came out most-significant first; the representation is little-endian.
    var q = []
    var m = qms.length - 1
    while (m >= 0) {
        q.push(qms[m])
        m = m - 1
    }
    return {q: pyABigNorm(q), r: pyABigNorm(rem)}
}

// Go's big.Int.Quo: TRUNCATED toward zero, so the sign is the sign of the product.
function pyABigQuo(x, y) {
    return pyABigNew(x["neg"] != y["neg"], pyABigDivMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])["q"])
}

// Go's big.Int.Rem: the remainder takes the sign of the DIVIDEND.
function pyABigRem(x, y) {
    return pyABigNew(x["neg"], pyABigDivMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])["r"])
}

// Go: big.Int.String().
function pyABigStr(v) {
    var limbs = v["limbs"]
    var out = "" + limbs[limbs.length - 1]
    var i = limbs.length - 2
    while (i >= 0) {
        var s = "" + limbs[i]
        while (s.length < 4) { s = "0" + s }
        out = out + s
        i = i - 1
    }
    if (v["neg"]) { return "-" + out }
    return out
}

// Go: big.Int.SetString(text, 0) - see the note above on why only base 10 with
// an optional sign (and the '_' separators Python allows, which the emitter has
// already stripped) is accepted.
function pyABigFromStr(text) {
    var s = text
    var neg = false
    var i = 0
    if (s.charAt(0) == "-") {
        neg = true
        i = 1
    } else {
        if (s.charAt(0) == "+") { i = 1 }
    }
    var digits = ""
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(i)
        if (c != "_") {
            if (c < "0" || c > "9") { pyFail("invalid BigInt literal " + text) }
            digits = digits + c
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (digits.length == 0) { pyFail("invalid BigInt literal " + text) }
    var limbs = []
    var end = digits.length
    while (end > 0) {
        var from = end - 4
        if (from < 0) { from = 0 }
        limbs.push(parseInt(digits.substring(from, end), 10))
        end = from
    }
    return pyABigNew(neg, limbs)
}

// An INTEGRAL, FINITE double as an exact bignum - Go's big.NewFloat(f).Int(nil),
// which bigEqFloat uses. An integral double above 2^53 is a multiple of 2, so
// halving it down to a 53 bit mantissa and doubling the bignum back up loses
// nothing (the same argument ruby-rt.metajs's rbExactDec makes).
function pyABigFromNum(n) {
    var neg = n < 0
    var m = Math.abs(n)
    var e = 0
    while (m > 9007199254740992) {
        m = m / 2
        e = e + 1
    }
    var limbs = []
    var x = m
    while (x > 0) {
        var q = Math.floor(x / 10000)
        limbs.push(x - q * 10000)
        x = q
    }
    if (limbs.length == 0) { limbs.push(0) }
    var ls = limbs
    var k = 0
    while (k < e) {
        ls = pyABigMulSmall(ls, 2)
        k = k + 1
    }
    return pyABigNew(neg, ls)
}

// Go: bigEqFloat (jsrt.go:8650). Only an integral, finite double can equal an
// arbitrary precision int. `f - f != 0` is the finiteness test: it is NaN for
// both an infinity and a NaN and exactly 0 for everything else - there is no
// isFinite and no exponent literal in this dialect.
function pyABigEqFloat(x, f) {
    if (f - f != 0) { return false }
    if (f != Math.trunc(f)) { return false }
    return pyABigCmp(x, pyABigFromNum(f)) == 0
}

// Go: bigArith (jsrt.go:2724), including bigPair's rule that BOTH operands must
// be bigs - a big meeting a double falls back to the double path, where the big
// converts to NaN. Answers null for "not applicable", never a value, so the
// caller can tell it apart from a legitimate result.
function pyABigArith(op, l, r) {
    if (!pyIsBig(l)) { return null }
    if (!pyIsBig(r)) { return null }
    if (op == "+") { return pyABigAdd(l, r) }
    if (op == "-") { return pyABigSub(l, r) }
    if (op == "*") { return pyABigMul(l, r) }
    if (op == "/") {
        if (pyABigIsZero(r)) { return null } // Let the double path produce Inf/NaN.
        return pyABigQuo(l, r)
    }
    if (op == "%") {
        // Unreachable from Python (js_pybin's "%" is pyFloorMod on doubles), but
        // bigArith has the arm and the division already answers both halves.
        if (pyABigIsZero(r)) { return null }
        return pyABigRem(l, r)
    }
    return null
}

// ============================================================================
// The shared scalar helpers  (Go: rt.truthy, rt.typeOf, rt.strictEq, jsCompare,
// pyEqual, pyFloorMod)
// ============================================================================

// Go: rt.truthy (jsrt.go:~910). A big is truthy when its sign is not 0.
function pyATruthy(v) {
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return v != 0 && v == v }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v.length > 0 }
    if (pyIsBig(v)) { return !pyABigIsZero(v) }
    return true
}

// Go: rt.typeOf (jsrt.go:1523) - the JAVASCRIPT typeof, which is what the two
// failure messages of js_pybin and js_pysum interpolate. It is NOT pyTypeName.
function pyAJsTypeOf(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return "undefined" }
    if (v === null) { return "object" }
    if (pyIsBig(v)) { return "bigint" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return "boolean" }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "number" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return "string" }
    if (typeof v == "function") { return "function" }
    return "object"
}

// Go: rt.strictEq (jsrt.go:1164), narrowed to the Python value model - none of
// the Char / boxed-double / sized-integer arms is reachable from Python. Two
// DISTINCT bigs of the same value are equal (Go compares them with Cmp), so
// `x is y` on two big literals of the same value is true in both engines.
function pyAStrictEq(a, b) {
    if (pyIsBig(a) || pyIsBig(b)) {
        if (pyIsBig(a) && pyIsBig(b)) { return pyABigCmp(a, b) == 0 }
        return false
    }
    return a === b
}

// Go: rt.jsCompare (jsrt.go:1490). This is a deliberate SECOND copy of the
// prelude's pyCompare, for two reasons the prelude cannot be assumed to have:
// the bigPair arm (both operands big -> Cmp; a big against a double falls into
// toNumber, which is NaN for a big, so EVERY ordering against a mixed pair is
// false in Go) and the answer 2 for NaN, which is what makes <, >, <= and >=
// all false at once.
function pyACompare(a, b) {
    if (typeof a == "string" && typeof b == "string") {
        if (a < b) { return -1 }
        if (a > b) { return 1 }
        return 0
    }
    if (pyIsBig(a) && pyIsBig(b)) { return pyABigCmp(a, b) }
    var an = pyNum(a)
    var bn = pyNum(b)
    if (an !== an || bn !== bn) { return 2 }
    if (an < bn) { return -1 }
    if (an > bn) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// Go: rt.pyEqual (jsrt.go:8580). The set / list / dict arms are the prelude's
// pyEqual; the two bigint arms come FIRST here only because a __pybig is a plain
// object and the prelude's pyEqual has no reason to know about it. A big against
// anything that is neither a big nor a number falls through to strictEq in Go,
// which is false, so this answers false directly.
function pyAEqual(x, y) {
    if (pyIsBig(x)) {
        if (pyIsBig(y)) { return pyABigCmp(x, y) == 0 }
        if (pyIsNum(y)) { return pyABigEqFloat(x, y) }
        return false
    }
    if (pyIsBig(y)) {
        if (pyIsNum(x)) { return pyABigEqFloat(y, x) }
        return false
    }
    return pyEqual(x, y)
}

// Go: pyFloorMod (jsrt.go:9403). Python's % takes the SIGN OF THE DIVISOR.
// math.Mod is fmod, which JavaScript's % is exactly - and, unlike
// `x - math.Floor(x/y)*y`, it is not the expression clang refuses to fuse, so
// this is a byte-for-byte match rather than an approximation.
function pyAFloorMod(x, y) {
    var r = x % y
    if (r != 0 && (r < 0) != (y < 0)) { return r + y }
    return r
}

// ============================================================================
// Go: rt.jsAdd (jsrt.go:1124)
// ============================================================================
function pyAJsAdd(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return pyToStr(l) + pyToStr(r) }
    // THE ONE REORDERING, and the exclusion that goes with it. Go reaches
    // bigArith AFTER the array/object arms because a *jsBigInt is a value of its
    // own and never a *jsObject; here a big IS a plain object, so the bigint arm
    // is taken FIRST (a big + a big has to answer a big, not a concatenation)
    // and the object arms then have to EXCLUDE one, or a MIXED pair would
    // concatenate where Go falls through.
    //
    // Falling through is the whole point: bigArith needs BOTH operands to be
    // bigints (bigPair, jsrt.go:2710), and rt.toNumber has no *jsBigInt arm, so
    // `0 + 10000000000000000000000000001` is NaN in the Go twin. Measured on the
    // differential probe, where this file answered the concatenation
    // '010000000000000000000000000001' instead.
    var big = pyABigArith("+", l, r)
    if (big !== null) { return big }
    if (pyIsArr(l) || pyIsArr(r)) { return pyToStr(l) + pyToStr(r) }
    if (pyIsBig(l) || pyIsBig(r)) { return pyNum(l) + pyNum(r) }
    if (pyIsObj(l) || pyIsObj(r)) { return pyToStr(l) + pyToStr(r) }
    return pyNum(l) + pyNum(r)
}

