// ----- js_is_type (jsrt.go:5266 -> rt.isTypeName, jsrt.go:8432) -------------
// `v is T`, the language-neutral spelling. The jsChar arm of the Go twin is
// Kotlin's and is unreachable from Python, which has no character type; the
// rest is the Go function line for line. The name switch is the JVM/Kotlin
// vocabulary, not Python's - the Python grammars do not emit this extern at
// all, and it is here because -rt-lib must export every language-neutral
// symbol of the table.
// js_is_type is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkIsChar/rtkIsArr hooks.
// ============================================================================
// CHUNK D -- classes, attributes, calls and exceptions.
//
// Ports, function for function, from abnf/jsrt.go:
//     js_pyclass_new  (7193)   js_pyclass_fill (7220)   js_pygetattr   (7234)
//     js_pysetattr    (7239)   js_pycall       (7256)   js_pysig       (7284)
//     js_pytype       (7345)   js_pyisinst     (7353)   js_pybuiltincls(7788)
//     js_pyexc        (5273)   js_pyexcmatch   (7501)   js_pyexcsplit  (7515)
//     js_pyenter      (7657)   js_pyexit       (7666)   js_pytruthy    (6273)
// plus the helpers rt.pyBindCall (9486), rt.pyMethodCall (9207),
// rt.callPyValue (9260), rt.pyIsInstance (9280), pyLinearize (9010),
// pyRootExcType (9593) and rt.pyExcGroupLeaves (9620).
//
// js_pyannot is LOWERED IN THE EMITTER and is deliberately absent.
// js_supercall is NOT emitted by languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf
// (`grep -n js_supercall` on it is empty), so it is deliberately absent too.
//
// Every helper carries the `pyD` prefix, EXCEPT `pyBindCall`, which the other
// chunks call by that exact name.
//
// ONE DIVERGENCE THAT APPLIES TO THIS WHOLE FILE, stated once here rather than
// at forty sites: the Go twin distinguishes "the property is ABSENT" from "the
// property HOLDS undefined" (`v, ok := o.props[name]`). A -rt-lib module has no
// hasOwn - the floor seeds no such builtin - so "present" is spelled
// `!== undefined` throughout. The only value that behaves differently is an
// attribute explicitly holding undefined, and undefined is Python's None in
// this value model, which every caller here treats the same way as an absent
// attribute anyway.
// ============================================================================

// ----- Python truthiness (Go: js_pytruthy, jsrt.go:6273) --------------------

// A RAW RETURN. languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf sets
// `core.truthyExt = "js_pytruthy"` (line 3312) and compile-core's truthy()
// emits `icmp ne <call>, 0` straight on that extern's result, so it must answer
// a RAW INTEGER rather than a handle. The `__raw` suffix is what tells
// languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf's export scanner to export the symbol as
// `js_pytruthy` and put this function's result through the floor's own
// js_truthy first. Without it a MetaJS `false` would come back as the HANDLE of
// false, which is 2, and 2 != 0, so EVERY condition in the program would be
// taken - silently. See docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, item 2.
function js_pytruthy__raw(v) {
    var els = pySetElemsOf(v)
    if (els !== null) { return els.length > 0 }
    if (pyIsArr(v)) { return v.length > 0 }
    if (pyIsDict(v)) { return v["keys"].length > 0 }
    return pyDTruthy(v)
}

// Go's rt.truthy (jsrt.go:909) for the Python value model. The boxed float /
// sized-integer / Java-double arms of the Go switch are unreachable from
// Python (its int and float are both the plain number), and the *jsBigInt arm
// is `t.v.Sign() != 0` - which here has to be read off the {__pybig} shape
// chunk A owns, because a plain object would otherwise take the `default: true`
// arm and `if 0n:` would be taken. The prelude has no big-sign helper, so the
// limbs are scanned directly.
function pyDTruthy(v) {
    if (pyIsNone(v)) { return false }
    if (pyIsBool(v)) { return v }
    // Go's `t != 0 && t == t` - the second half is the NaN test.
    if (pyIsNum(v)) { return v != 0 && v == v }
    // Go's `len(t) > 0` is a BYTE length, and byteLen(s) == 0 exactly when the
    // code-unit length is 0, so the two agree on emptiness.
    if (pyIsStr(v)) { return v.length > 0 }
